# Famous Books and Authors: From the Vedas to the Booker Shelf

> Ask a general-knowledge examiner to name the most reliable question
> ever written, and they will point at a book-author pair. The Vedas
> and the epics, the Mughal chronicles, the freedom movement's
> classics, and the modern prize-winners form a single ladder of
> Indian writing, with the world's classics bolted on. This lesson
> climbs the ladder once, carefully.

## 1. The Ancient Canon — Vedas, Upanishads, Epics

[definition]
The **four Vedas** — Rig, Sama, Yajur, and Atharva — are India's
oldest sacred texts. The **Rig Veda** is the oldest; its famous
Gayatri Mantra appears in it. Each Veda has attached prose
commentaries: Brahmanas, Aranyakas, and the **Upanishads**, of which
108 are traditionally counted with about 13 regarded as principal.
[/definition]

| Text | Author / seer | Exam point |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Rig Veda | — | Oldest Veda; Gayatri Mantra |
| *Satyameva Jayate* source | — | Mundaka Upanishad |
| Ramayana | Valmiki | 7 kandas, 24,000 verses (trad.) |
| Mahabharata | Ved Vyasa | 18 parvas; longest poem |
| Bhagavad Gita | — | Part of the Bhishma Parva |
| Arthashastra | Kautilya (Chanakya) | Treatise on statecraft; Mauryan-era |
| Panchatantra | Vishnu Sharma | Fables |
| Abhijnana Shakuntalam, Meghaduta | Kalidasa | Sanskrit drama's summit |
| Charaka Samhita | Charaka | Medicine (Ayurveda) |
| Sushruta Samhita | Sushruta | Surgery |
| Natyashastra | Bharata Muni | Dramaturgy — the "fifth Veda" of theatre |
| Rajatarangini | Kalhana | History of Kashmir (12th c.) |
| Ashtadhyayi | Panini | Sanskrit grammar |

The pairing pattern in questions runs text-to-author and
text-to-subject: *Arthashastra* pairs with statecraft, the
*Natyashastra* with dance and drama, the *Charaka Samhita* with
medicine. When a paper offers "Vishnu Sharma — medicine" as an
option, it is mixing the fable-writer with the physician; the
matching answer for medicine is Charaka.

## 2. Medieval, Mughal, and Regional Classics

| Book | Author | Note |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Baburnama | Babur | Autobiography, in Chagatai Turkish |
| Humayunama | Gulbadan Begum | By a woman of the Mughal house |
| Akbarnama, Ain-i-Akbari | Abul Fazl | History + administrative gazette of Akbar's reign |
| Tuzuk-i-Jahangiri | Jahangir | Memoirs |
| Padshahnama | Abdul Hamid Lahori | Shah Jahan's reign |
| Prithviraj Raso | Chand Bardai | Semi-legendary epic of Prithviraj Chauhan |
| Ramcharitmanas | Tulsidas | Awadhi retelling of the Ramayana |
| Guru Granth Sahib | Compiled by Guru Arjan Dev (and Guru Gobind Singh additions) | Sikh scripture |
| Mitakshara | Vijnaneshwara | Hindu law commentary (12th c.) |

The Mughal memoirs are a clean question family: Babur (Baburnama),
Humayun (Gulbadan's Humayunama), Akbar (Akbarnama — by his courtier
Abul Fazl, not the emperor himself), and Jahangir (his own
Tuzuk-i-Jahangiri). The trap option "Akbarnama by Akbar" is wrong —
emperors Babur and Jahangir wrote their own; Akbar did not.

## 3. The Modern Indian Shelf

| Book | Author | Exam angle |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Gitanjali | Rabindranath Tagore | Nobel 1913 |
| Anandamath | Bankim Chandra Chattopadhyay | Contains *Vande Mataram* |
| Hind Swaraj, *My Experiments with Truth* | M.K. Gandhi | Autobiography title is a question in itself |
| Discovery of India, *An Autobiography* | Jawaharlal Nehru | Written in Ahmednagar Fort prison |
| India Wins Freedom | Maulana Abul Kalam Azad | Partition account |
| Wings of Fire | A.P.J. Abdul Kalam (with Arun Tiwari) | Autobiography of the missile scientist |
| Godan, Gaban | Munshi Premchand | Hindi realism |
| Malgudi Days | R.K. Narayan | Fictional South Indian town |
| A Suitable Boy | Vikram Seth | One of the longest single-volume English novels |
| Midnight's Children | Salman Rushdie | Booker of Bookers |
| The God of Small Things | Arundhati Roy | Booker 1997 |
| The White Tiger | Aravind Adiga | Booker 2008 |
| Train to Pakistan | Khushwant Singh | Partition novel |
| The Argumentative Indian | Amartya Sen | Essays |
| Everyone Loves a Good Drought | P. Sainath | Rural reporting |
| We, the People / Courtroom genius... | N.A. Palkhivala | Constitutional essays |

## 4. The World Classics and the Reference Shelf

| Book | Author | Field |
| --- | --- | --- |
| The Republic | Plato | Philosophy |
| The Prince | Machiavelli | Political strategy |
| The Wealth of Nations | Adam Smith | Economics (1776) |
| Das Kapital; *Communist Manifesto* | Karl Marx (Manifesto with Engels) | Political economy |
| Origin of Species | Charles Darwin | Evolution |
| Principia Mathematica | Isaac Newton | Mechanics |
| Relativity (popular exposition) | Albert Einstein | Physics |
| The Iliad, The Odyssey | Homer | Greek epic |
| Divine Comedy | Dante | Italian epic |
| Don Quixote | Cervantes | Often called the first modern novel |
| Hamlet, Macbeth | Shakespeare | Drama |
| War and Peace | Tolstoy | Russian epic |
| David Copperfield, *Oliver Twist* | Charles Dickens | Victorian fiction |
| Pride and Prejudice | Jane Austen | Regency fiction |
| Mein Kampf | Adolf Hitler | Autobiography/manifesto |
| Uncle Tom's Cabin | Harriet Beecher Stowe | Anti-slavery novel |
| The Story of My Experiments... — (world analogue) | — | — |
| Harry Potter series | J.K. Rowling | Modern bestseller |

Science-economics pairs make the most reliable world-set questions:
*Origin of Species* goes with Darwin and evolution, *The Wealth of
Nations* with Adam Smith and the invisible hand, *Das Kapital* with
Marx. The 1776 coincidence — Smith's book and the American
Declaration of Independence — is a dating trap papers enjoy.

[example]
A previous-year pattern: "Who wrote *India Wins Freedom*?" with
Nehru and Gandhi as the trap options; the answer is Maulana Azad.
The companion: "*Discovery of India* was written by —" (Nehru),
written in prison between 1942 and 1946. The third in the set:
"*Wings of Fire* is the autobiography of —" (A.P.J. Abdul Kalam).
Freedom-movement memoirs form a closed set of four names — Gandhi,
Nehru, Azad, Kalam — and the paper simply rotates the pairing.
[/example]

[summary]
Ancient set: Rig Veda oldest; Mundaka Upanishad behind *Satyameva
Jayate*; Valmiki's Ramayana and Vyasa's Mahabharata (18 parvas);
Kautilya's Arthashastra; Kalidasa's Shakuntalam; Charaka and
Sushruta for medicine and surgery; Kalhana's Rajatarangini.
Medieval: Baburnama (Babur), Humayunama (Gulbadan), Akbarnama and
Ain-i-Akbari (Abul Fazl), Tuzuk-i-Jahangiri, Ramcharitmanas
(Tulsidas). Modern: Gitanjali (Tagore), Anandamath (Bankim), the
Nehru–Gandhi–Azad–Kalam memoir set, Godan (Premchand), the Booker
shelf (Roy, Rushdie, Adiga, Vikram Seth), P. Sainath's rural
reporting. World: Republic (Plato), Prince (Machiavelli), Wealth of
Nations (Smith), Das Kapital (Marx), Origin of Species (Darwin),
Iliad–Odyssey (Homer), Don Quixote (Cervantes), War and Peace
(Tolstoy), Uncle Tom's Cabin (Stowe).
[/summary]
